Project: {project}
Suite: {suite}
Exit code: {exit_code}
Environment signals: {env_signals}

Below is the failure context. The line marked '▶' is the detected failure trigger; lines around it form the error body.

{failure_text}

Label definitions:
{label_definitions}

Your task:
1. Pick exactly ONE label from: {labels}.
   - Only 'acceptable' when there is a clear assertion/logic failure in project code.
   - Only 'false_positive' when there is clearly NO real failure.
   - Otherwise, and whenever you are unsure, pick 'problematic' (our environment's fault).
2. Set 'confidence': 'high' (clear diagnostic signature), 'medium' (likely, minor ambiguity), or 'low' (guessing / defaulting per the rule above).
3. Write a short 'reason'.
4. Propose a 'rule_body' as a COMPACT REGEX PATTERN (Python re syntax) matching the failure's ERROR / DIAGNOSTIC signature (exception type, error message, the lines below the '▶' trigger). It must match the CLASS of error, NOT a specific test file path (e.g. 'test/foo.test.ts') or test name/title (e.g. 'should handle X') -- do not build the pattern from file paths or test identifiers. Replace other volatile tokens with placeholders: line numbers, ports, PIDs, hashes, versions, sizes -> '\d+' / '[0-9a-f]+'; embedded paths/timestamps inside the error text -> '.*'. Keep the stable DIAGNOSTIC CORE literal (e.g. 'TypeError: Cannot read properties of .*', 'Cannot find module .*'). Do NOT escape regex metacharacters yourself -- write a real pattern. Avoid patterns so broad they match unrelated failures (no bare '.*', no single common word). If there is genuinely no stable ERROR pattern (only a test name/path varies), use null.

Respond with exactly this JSON and nothing else:
{"label": <one of labels>, "confidence": "high|medium|low", "reason": <string>, "rule_body": <regex string or null>}
